pancake is a *codec-neutral* CapTP core: the session reasons in messages, and a
codec turns those messages into bytes and back (the
[Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) page sets up that seam). Two ship today.
[OCapN](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn) settles the wire format as
[Syrup](https://github.com/ocapn/syrup), and pancake speaks Syrup wherever the
protocol calls for it; [Cap'n Proto](https://capnproto.org/) is the second codec, a
fixed-width, word-aligned alternative to Syrup's variable-length byte records.

[One message, two spellings. Syrup lays it out as variable-length byte records and Cap'n Proto as fixed 8-byte words; decode either and you get back the same message.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-codec.svg'))

## What a codec is, and is not

The session deals in messages, a deliver, a resolve, a promise, and never in
bytes. A codec only reads and writes bytes; it never decides what a message *means*
or whether it is *allowed*. That authority stays in the session, so changing codecs
changes the wire and never the behavior. Decode the same message through either
codec and you get the same borrowed view; encode through either and the bytes come
back equivalent. A build check holds that line: the deliver/resolve and
bootstrap-fetch scenarios produce identical observable traces on both codecs, even
though the two wire encodings look nothing alike.

## The two that ship

| Codec | Wire shape | Trade | Reach for it when |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Syrup | length-prefixed byte records | compact frames; Goblins-canonical | talking to Goblins, or when per-connection density matters |
| Cap'n Proto | 8-byte-aligned words | fixed-offset fields, larger frames | two pancake peers, once you have measured the encode/decode trade |

### Syrup

The Goblins-canonical profile, and the format OCapN requires on the wire. It is
what pancake speaks by default and what it speaks to any
[Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) peer. Its records are
variable-length, which keeps frames compact.

### Cap'n Proto

A second, hand-rolled codec, selectable on a live session. Its word-aligned frames
are larger on the wire, with every field at a fixed offset. Both peers settle on it at
handshake (`pairedWithCodec` in Zig, `pancake_peer_new_negotiated` over the C ABI, and
the matching idiom in every binding), and from there the whole conversation rides it.
Whether that shape pays off for your traffic is a measured question, not a claimed one:
`zig build bench-codec` reports the current encode and decode ns/op and MB/s for both,
and the [Performance]($link.page('performance')) page is where those numbers live.

## Bring your own

The set is open on purpose. The two pancake ships are the two it needs today, but
the codec layer is a registry, not a fixed switch. A Zig consumer declares
`pub const pancake_codecs` in the root source file, binds a wire id to an
implementation, and the same core speaks it with no change to the session above. It
all resolves at compile time, so a third codec costs the dispatch nothing the two
built-ins did not.
